DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     mmkkpprroottoo builds a complete _p_r_o_t_o description file, of the
     kind read by mmkkffss, by scanning _d_e_v_i_c_e for bad blocks and in-
     serting the resulting %bb bad-block lines into an otherwise
     complete proto file read from the standard input.

     The standard input must already contain the boot/name/pack
     line and the block-count line of the proto file (see mmkkffss);
     mmkkpprroottoo copies these two lines through unchanged to the
     standard output.  It then opens _d_e_v_i_c_e and reads it one
     block at a time, from block 0 up to the block count just
     read; for each block that cannot be read, it emits the block
     number in a %bb line, exactly as if the bad block had been
     listed by hand in a proto file.

     After the bad-block lines, mmkkpprroottoo copies the rest of the
     standard input (the directory and file specifications)
     through to the standard output unchanged.  If the standard
     input is exhausted immediately after the block-count line,
     that is, it specifies no root directory at all, mmkkpprroottoo ap-
     pends a minimal root directory specification of its own:

          d--755 3 1
          $

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
     Cannot open %s
          The named _d_e_v_i_c_e could not be opened for reading.

     Unexpected end of file on proto input
          The standard input ended before mmkkpprroottoo had read both
          the boot/name/pack line and the block-count line.

     Preposterous block size: %D
          The block count given on the standard input is less
          than four.

     Bad super block
          Block 1, where the super block must live, cannot be
          read.

     Bad root inode block
          Block 2, the first i-node block, cannot be read.

